meta {
  name: Login as CRM Manager
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{ownerEmail}}",
    "password": "{{password}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setVar("crmAccessToken", res.getBody().accessToken);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("the caller actually holds crm_service", function () {
    // The point of this request. Without it the whole folder 403s on the
    // permission guard and every assertion below fails for a reason that has
    // nothing to do with what it is testing.
    const perms = res.getBody().user.permissions;
    expect(perms).to.include("crm_service:read");
    expect(perms).to.include("crm_service:write");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /auth/login — a caller who can work the service desk

  Captured as `crmAccessToken`, under its **own** name: overwriting the
  collection-level `accessToken` would silently re-run every later folder as
  this user and invalidate the `own`-scope assertions in Leads, Performance and
  Leaderboard.

  ## Why the owner rather than the producer

  The collection's default bearer is the **producer**, and a producer holds no
  `crm_service` permission at all — deliberately. `DEFAULT_ROLE_TEMPLATES` gives
  `crm_service:read`/`:write` to **CSR**, **CRM** and **Agency Owner** only, and
  the demo seed's owner is the one account with a password this collection
  knows. That is also the reason `POST /leads/:id/service-ticket` exists as its
  own endpoint under `/leads`: the producer who presses Start Quote could not
  call the CRM controller at all.

  The owner's `agency` data scope is wider than a CSR's, which does not weaken
  anything here — nothing in this folder asserts a scope clamp.
}
